// A line of `Log` at a level that is off, in a loop that does little else: a billion
// `Log.debug($"...")` calls while the level is `Info`. The compiler puts each inside
// `if Log.isOn(LogLevel.Debug)` (choice 1447), so the text is never built: what is left is the
// load of the level, a static found the first time it is read (one load and one branch more),
// and a compare. The C keeps the level in a plain atomic global and does the same test.
void main() {
    long sum = 0
    for i in 0..1000000000 {
        Log.debug($"step {i}, sum {sum}")
        sum = sum.wrappingAdd(i ^ (sum >> 3))
    }
    print(sum)
}
